&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.25in;text-align:justify;line-height:
117%'>&lt;i>“Everyone that puts away his wife—except for sexual immorality—treats*
her like an adulteress, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.”&lt;/i>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.25in;text-align:justify;line-height:
117%'>&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.25in;text-align:justify;line-height:
117%'>            For purposes here, we pass over some technicalities
associated with the aorist passive infinitive (&lt;span lang=EL>μοιχευθῆναι&lt;/span>,
&lt;i>moicheuthēnai&lt;/i>, &lt;i>“makes her to be adulterated[?]”&lt;/i>) and deal with more
straightforward considerations. &lt;/p></description></item><item><title>ETHICS AND THE GOLDEN RULE</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/ethics/ethics-and-the-golden-rule/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/ethics/ethics-and-the-golden-rule/</guid><description>&lt;style>
/* Font Definitions */
